WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.070
If you're writing
about people, you

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.115
have to be interested in people.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.430
What makes a question
a good question?

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:12.270
Well, I like a question
like, do you children shower?

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.460
Who is the drunkest customer
that you've had today?

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.510
I met this woman one
time, and I said,

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:18.400
when was the last time
you touched a monkey?

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.430
And she said, oh, can
you smell it on me?

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.400
In this class, I'll talk
about connecting with people

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.170
and asking them questions.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:28.760
I'll talk about keeping a diary.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.290
It begins with a notebook.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.450
I'll be reading a few
of my published essays.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:34.220
"Lisa, you're a natural."

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.900
I'm often asked,
what does your family

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.340
feel when you write about them?

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.190
Well, I thought I would bring
my sister Lisa here and find out

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:40.883
from her.

00:00:40.883 --> 00:00:41.612
[LAUGHS]

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.710
I go willingly to my desk every
morning and write in my diary.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:52.940
I write something over
probably 12 to 18 times.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.100
Writing is rewriting.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.310
And when you read in front of an
audience, and I'll make notes,

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:00.650
and I'll rewrite it, read
it, rewrite it, read it,

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:04.400
rewrite it to get an idea
of the overall rhythm.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.163
The danger is writing
something that just stops

00:01:08.163 --> 00:01:09.455
instead of something that ends.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.820
A huge part of writing is
learning to trust yourself.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.370
And that just comes
with experience.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:20.360
I wrote every day for 15 years
before my first book came out.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.790
Most of the writers that I
know have similar stories.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.600
Write something that stinks.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.310
Try and fail.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:29.780
Failure is fantastic.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.510
Don't underestimate failure.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.510
You have to connect with
people on some level.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.330
And it's good to pay
attention and keep

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.510
a diary because,
every now and then,

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:47.110
your life feels like a story.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.268
It's exciting to
think that you're

00:01:49.268 --> 00:01:51.310
living in this world with
all these other people.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.900
And today, is an
opportunity for you

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.130
to connect with some of them.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.880
That makes life worth living.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.320
I'm David Sedaris, and
this is my MasterClass.

